  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct experimental evidence (animal)** – Genetic or antibody‑mediated blockade of IL‑17 or its upstream cytokine IL‑23 dampens disease in mouse models of experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis and collagen‑induced arthritis, showing that IL‑17‑producing Th17 cells are required for tissue‑inflammatory pathology【PMID:27576555】. A fate‑mapping study of intestinal Th17 cells demonstrated that a stem‑like IL‑17⁺ population gives rise to GM‑CSF⁺/IFN‑γ⁺ encephalitogenic cells that drive CNS autoimmunity, directly linking IL‑17‑expressing cells to disease‑causing effectors【PMID:34875227】 (animal).\n\n**Human association** – Elevated IL‑17A levels and increased frequencies of IL‑17‑producing CD4⁺, γδ‑T, NKT and ILC3 cells have been reported in the blood or lesions of patients with multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, ankylosing spondylitis, systemic lupus erythematosus and other autoimmune disorders, establishing a robust correlation between IL‑17 presence and disease activity【PMID:37670700】 (human observational).\n\n**Therapeutic validation (human)** – FDA‑approved monoclonal antibodies that neutralize IL‑17A (secukinumab, ixekizumab) or block the IL‑17 receptor (brodalumab) produce marked clinical improvement in psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis and hidradenitis suppurativa, providing functional proof that IL‑17 drives pathogenic inflammation in these autoimmune diseases【PMID:39875232】 (human interventional).\n\n**Mechanistic context** – Reviews of the IL‑23/IL‑17 axis describe how Th17 cell plasticity, RORγt post‑translational modifications, and interactions with innate cells amplify IL‑17‑mediated tissue damage, while also noting that IL‑17 can be protective in host defense, underscoring disease‑specific effects【PMID:25145755】【PMID:34635393】 (human & animal mechanistic synthesis).\n\n**Key study‑context limitations** – Most primary data come from mouse models that may not fully recapitulate human disease heterogeneity; IL‑17 blockade is highly effective in skin and joint diseases but shows limited benefit in inflammatory bowel disease, indicating tissue‑specific redundancy with other cytokines; reviews synthesize existing literature without new experimental controls, and the pathogenic role of IL‑17 can be confounded by its protective antimicrobial functions, requiring careful interpretation of causality【PMID:27576555】【PMID:39875232】.\n\n*Overall, experimental, observational and therapeutic data support IL‑17 as a promoter of autoimmune disease, but translational relevance is tempered by model‑specific and tissue‑specific constraints.*\n\nGraph-derived candidate insights\n- PATH decd42689d2018ff — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; direct graph evidence; HOP 1: il-17 promotes autoimmune disease [PMID: 27576555]."
  },
